From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 01:50:00 -0400
Subject: sidebar: the rail's rows breathe, the title row is its glyph, the switcher's stack measures itself

The collapsed rail on the Windows preview, three minutes in (round 9,
docs/ARC-ROUND2.md):

- The 44 DIP rows sat 2 DIP apart and read as one strip. 6 DIP in the
  rail, in the unpinned container's and the folder's layouts, keyed on
  the width being laid out like the rest of the rail form (sidebar Y12).
- The Space title row showed its glyph and the name elided to "...",
  left in the column. The glyph alone now, centred and a size up; the
  strip keeps the glyph and the name apart, so the open sidebar gets
  both back (Y13).
- The switcher's stack kept a fixed height that counted every chip full
  while the inactive ones were 6 DIP dots, and the row it sits in kept a
  height set by hand at construction and at each collapse, so a Space
  added in the rail pushed the "+" past the window's bottom until the
  sidebar was opened and collapsed again. The stack goes by the count --
  full chips to four Spaces, dots past that, and the pointer does not
  grow the dots -- measures itself in ApplyOverflow, and the row takes
  its height from its layout, with the switcher's flex (the open row's
  width) off in the rail: a BoxLayout with a flexible child reports the
  space it is offered as its preferred size, and left on it handed the
  row everything under the Archived row (Y13, docs/HANDOFF.md trap 43).
- The floating Space name, placed above the chip, covered the downloads
  button in the rail. None there; the chip's tooltip names the Space
  (Y14).

Why: docs/features/sidebar.md Y12-Y14.
Removable when: the rail gets a layout of its own instead of the open
sidebar's views in a narrower form.
